  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nOur preview of September-like weather will continue today,\nthough temps will likely be a couple degrees warmer than\nThursday, but dewpoints are likely to remain unusually low for\nlate July. One difference for today is the expectation of more\ncloudiness this morning as a fairly pronounced shortwave trough\nmoves across the region. Majority of the associated ascent with\nthis feature is going toward saturating the parched atmosphere,\nthough rather strong and deep f-gen circulation has been \neffective at allowing a band of some sprinkles or very light \nrain showers to actually reach the ground across Iowa. This \nf-gen band is progged to translate eastward into northern \nIllinois this morning and certainly can't rule out some \nsprinkles making it eastward into our area. The best chance of \nsprinkles looks to be roughly near and just south of the I-80 \ncorridor. \n\nIn the wake of this shortwave, skies should clear out from\nnorthwest to southeast this afternoon, though some high level\nsmoke noted upstream on satellite imagery Thursday could make it\ninto the area. The HRRR smoke guidance does show a bit of an\nincrease in vertically integrated smoke this afternoon, though\nsmoke looks to remain aloft and much less thick than last week,\nso only nudged sky cover up a bit to account for the smoke. \n\nAnother, convectively enhanced, shortwave trough is likely to\npass south of our area tonight into Saturday morning. Fairly\ndeep (300-315K surfaces) but modest isentropic ascent could\nresult in a few showers across mainly our southern CWA late\ntonight into Saturday morning. \n\nUpper ridge is progged to amplify over the central U.S. Sunday\ninto Monday, likely allowing for a quick shot of heat and\nhumidity. Evapotranspirationally-supplemented dewpoints appear\nlikely to surge well into the 70s and probably lower 80s in the\ndensest agricultural areas Sunday into Monday while nudging\ntemperatures down a bit from the NBM. Net result is Sunday heat\nindices peaking well into the 90s to slightly over 100 degrees \nin some areas, with more widespread 100 to locally over 105\ndegree heat indices possible Monday. \n\nThe caveat to the heat Sunday and Monday is the potential for\nconvection. Conceptually, a northwest flow pattern like the one\ncoming Sunday into Monday can produce robust MCS activity.\nHowever, the seedling low amplitude shortwaves that ultimately\ngo on to spawn strong MCSs and convectively enhanced vorts tend\nto be much too low amplitude to resolve at this distance. Often,\nthough not always, guidance at this distance would have a more\nrobust convective signal, but that certainly doesn't preclude\nsomething more coherent coming into sight in later runs.\nBarring any convectively enhanced shortwaves/MCS activity,\nforecast soundings Sunday and Monday exhibit a strong capping \ninversion, which would likely keep things dry. Opted to maintain\nslight chances for thunderstorms Sunday afternoon through\nMonday, to cover the potential for an upstream MCS developing\nand ripping southeast across our area. If a well timed MCS or \nMCS debris move across the area during this time frame, then \nthat would obviously have big implications on temperatures and \nheat potential. \n\nCold front and more of synoptic shortwave trough could result in\nbetter chances for thunderstorms Monday night followed by\nsomewhat cooler and less humid weather heading into the middle\nof next week. \n\n- Izzi",
